titre $ Geranium Belgique
com$ adapt GF mai 2016
rem$LANGUE$Anglais
1,0$ source : Manual of the Alien Plants of Belgium
1,30$ bibliographie
1,0$
1,0 $ Stamens at least twice as long as petals, long exserted $ G. macrorrhizum (incl. G. xcantabrigiense)
1,2 $ Stamens hardly or not exserted, at most 1,5x as long as petals
2,3 $ Petals abruptly narrowed at base to form a distinct claw about half or more the length of the petal
2,5 $ Petals gradually narrowed towards base without a claw or sometimes with a short claw less than half the length of the petal
3,0 $ Leaves divided ½-2/3 to base, nearly glabrous and shiny. Sepals distinctly keeled, glabrous (native) $ G. lucidum
3,4 $ Leaves divided nearly to base, usually hairy and rather dull. Sepals not keeled, glandular hairy
4,0 $ Mericarps with dense wrinkles in the basal half and 2-3(-4) deep collar-like ridges towards apex. Petals 5-9 mm long. Anthers yellow $ G. purpureum
4,0 $ Mericarps with sparse, shallow ribs in the basal half and with 0-1(-2) deep collar-like ridges towards apex. Petals 8-14 mm long. Anthers pinkish-orange to purplish (native) $ G. robertianum
5,6 $ Annuals, biennials or perennials (if perennial than without a distinct rhizome). Petals up to 10 mm long (often much shorter)
5,14 $ Perennials with stout rhizomes. Petals usually much longer (if shorter than blackish)
6,0 $ Fertile stamens 5 (outer whorl of stamens with filaments lacking anthers) (native) $ G. pusillum
6,7 $ Fertile stamens 10 (all filaments bearing anthers)
7,8 $ Mericarps reticulate (i.e. transversely ridged)
7,9 $ Mericarps smooth
8,0 $ Leaves angular in outline. Mericarps separating without stylar beak (fruit discharge mechanism not operative). Sepals distinctly mucronate (mucro ca. 1 mm long). Rostrum 5-8 mm long $ G. divaricatum
8,0 $ Leaves rotund in outline. Mericarps separating with long stylar beak (fruit discharge mechanism operative). Sepals indistinctly mucronate (mucro ca. 0,1-0,2 mm long). Rostrum 6-11 mm long (native) $ G. molle
9,0 $ Mericarps glabrous (native) $ G. aequale
9,10 $ Mericarps hairy (at least in part but often throughout)
10,11 $ Leaves divided ½-2/3 to base
10,12 $ Leaves divided nearly to base
11,0 $ Perennial. Petals 7-10 mm long, notched at apex $ G. pyrenaicum
11,0 $ Annual. Petals 5-7 mm long, rounded to truncate at apex, not notched (native) $ G. rotundifolium
12,0 $ Entire plant eglandular hairy. Peduncle slender, usually longer than 25 mm, longer than the subtending leaves. Petals bright pink, rounded at apex, 8-10 mm long (native) $ G. columbinum
12,13 $ Plant glandular hairy (especially peduncles). Peduncle stouter, usually shorter than 25 mm, shorter than the subtending leaves. Petals reddish purple or pale lilac, 4-6 mm long
13,0 $ Petals pale lilac, entire at apex. Mericarp blackish at maturity, 3-4,5 mm long. Rostrum of fruit 16-21 mm long $ G. carolinianum
13,0 $ Petals reddish purple, notched at apex. Mericarps brownish at maturity, 2-2,5 mm long. Rostrum of fruit 7-10 mm long (native) $ G. dissectum
14,0 $ All flowers solitary (native) $ G. sanguineum
14,15 $ Most flowers 2(-3) per peduncle
15,0 $ Petals very dark purplish to nearly black, apiculate at apex. Mericarps pointed at base $ G. phaeum
15,16 $ Petals pinkish, purplish, reddish or sometimes whitish but never blackish. Mericarps rounded at base
16,0 $ Petals deeply notched at apex. Leaves with 3-5 lobes, leaf lobes shallowly crenate $ G. nodosum
16,17 $ Petals rounded to slightly emarginate at apex, never deeply notched. Leaf lobes deeply dentate
17,18 $ Pedicels densely glandular hairy. Inflorescence compact (pedicels at most 3x as long as sepals)
17,19 $ Pedicels eglandular or with some sparse glandular hairs. Inflorescence lax (pedicels at least 4x as long as sepals)
18,0 $ Petals slightly emarginate at apex. Mericarps without tuft of bristles at base $ G. x magnificum
18,0 $ Petals rounded at apex. Mericarps with tuft of bristles at base (native) $ G. pratense and G. sylvaticum
19,0 $ Pedicels hirsute-hairy with coarse retrorse hairs. Fruiting pedicels curved near apex. Stamens with filaments shortly ciliate at base (native) $ G. palustre
19,0 $ Pedicels with slender, patent eglandular hairs intermixed with numerous very short, more or less glandular ones. Fruiting pedicels erect at apex. Stamens with filaments long ciliate throughout (except in upper 1/3) $ G. endressii (incl. G. xoxonianum)________________________________________

